course: world
lesson: 192
title: Olympiad: time and calendar puzzles ★★★
level: primary-4
stars: 3
explain: Time puzzles look like sums, but most of them hide one trap: a gap counted as a moment, a year 0 that never was, or a clock that jumped forward in the night. Spot the trap and the sum is easy.
explain: The week is a merry-go-round of 7 seats, so big jumps shrink: 100 days is 14 whole weeks and 2 days, and only the 2 days move the weekday on.
explain: Picture the church clock striking 6: bong, gap, bong, gap... There are 6 bongs but only 5 gaps, and the gaps are what take the time. It is the same fence-post trap as posts and rails.
explain: Calendars have their own traps too: 2100 will skip 29 February, the 21st century began in 2001, not 2000, and from 10 BC to AD 10 is 19 years, because no year 0 sits in the middle.
explain: People also stretch time when they talk. "I've waited forever" or "a million years" is not a number to use; the number that counts is the one on the clock, or the last one someone says after correcting themselves.
fact: 100 days is 14 weeks and 2 days, so 100 days after a Wednesday is a Friday.
fact: If the day before yesterday was Thursday, today is Saturday and the day after tomorrow is Monday.
fact: A clock striking 6 makes 5 gaps between its bongs; if that takes 5 seconds, each gap is 1 second, so striking 12, with 11 gaps, takes 11 seconds.
fact: A 24-hour digital clock shows four digits all the same 3 times a day: 00:00, 11:11 and 22:22.
fact: March has 31 days, which is 4 weeks and 3 days, so if 1 March is a Monday, 1 April is a Thursday.
fact: 2000 was a leap year because it divides by 400, but 2100 will not be one, though it divides by 4.
fact: Someone who is 9 now was 6 three years ago and will be 12 in three years, exactly twice 6.
fact: On the night the clocks go forward, going to bed at 22:00 and waking when the clock says 07:00 gives only 8 hours of sleep.
fact: There is no year 0, so a Roman born in 20 BC turned 39 on his birthday in AD 20.
fact: In winter New York is 5 hours behind London and Kyiv is 2 hours ahead of London, so Kyiv is 7 hours ahead of New York.
fact: A coin stamped "25 BC" must be a fake, because people living then could not know that a man would be born 25 years later and years would be counted from him.
fact: A sundial works only when the Sun casts a shadow, so it is no use at night or on a grey, cloudy day.
fact: Words people use: "fixin' to" (southern USA) means about to; "tamping" (Wales) means furious; "dreich" (Scotland) means grey and drizzly; "servo" (Australia) means a petrol station; "bday" is short for birthday.
exercise: Today is Wednesday. What day of the week will it be in 100 days? | Friday | Thursday | Saturday | Wednesday again
exercise: The day before yesterday was Thursday. What day will the day after tomorrow be? | Monday | Sunday | Saturday | Tuesday
exercise: A clock takes 5 seconds to strike 6 o'clock, with equal gaps. How long does it take to strike 12? | 11 seconds | 10 seconds | 12 seconds | 14 seconds
exercise: How many times a day does a 24-hour digital clock show four digits that are all the same? | 3 times: 00:00, 11:11, 22:22 | 2 times | 4 times | 6 times
exercise: 1 March is a Monday. What day is 1 April? | Thursday | Wednesday | Monday | Friday
exercise: Which of these years will have no 29 February? | 2100 | 2028 | 2032 | 2400
exercise: In 3 years Tom will be twice as old as he was 3 years ago. How old is Tom now? | 9 | 12 | 15 | 18
exercise: On the night the clocks go forward, Ava goes to bed at 22:00 and wakes when the clock says 07:00. How long did she sleep? | 8 hours | 9 hours | 10 hours | 7 hours and a half
exercise: A Roman was born in 20 BC. How old did he turn on his birthday in AD 20? | 39 | 40 | 41 | 20
exercise: In January it is 9 am in New York. What time is it in Kyiv? | 4 pm | 2 pm | 11 am | 7 pm
exercise: A film of 1 hour 50 minutes ends at 13:20. When did it start? | 11:30 | 11:10 | 12:30 | 11:50
exercise: A full moon is on 3 March. Roughly when is the full moon after the next one? | Around the start of May | Around 1 April | In mid-June | At the end of March
exercise: Swimming is every other Thursday, starting Thursday 5 March. When is the third swim? | Thursday 2 April | Thursday 26 March | Thursday 9 April | Thursday 19 March
exercise: Which year was the first year of the 21st century? | 2001 | 2000 | 2100 | 1999
exercise: Leah was born on 29 February 2008. How many real 29 February birthdays has she had by the end of 2028? | 5 | 20 | 10 | 21
test: Mia: "my bday was on a Wednesday this year and there's no 29 Feb before the next one, so it's a Wednesday every year innit." What is true? | Next year it moves on one day, to a Thursday | It stays on a Wednesday for good, as long as no 29 February comes | It jumps back to a Tuesday | It moves on a whole week to the next Wednesday
test: Nan: "I've told you a million times, the bus goes at 8:40! It's 8:25 — no, my watch is slow, it's 8:28." How long until the bus? | 12 minutes | 15 minutes | A million minutes, near enough | 8 minutes and 40 seconds
test: Jack, at the servo: "Waited literally forever for the car wash, like three hours. Well, 25 minutes tbh." He started waiting at 2:50. When did it end? | At 3:15 | At 5:50, three hours later | At 3:25 | At 3:05, twenty-five minutes before
test: Leo: "The clock did 6 bongs in 5 seconds, so 12 bongs is 10 seconds, easy." What has he missed? | Counting gaps: 6 bongs make 5 gaps, 12 make 11, so 11 seconds | Clocks always strike faster at noon, so 12 bongs is quicker | Nothing: 6 bongs in 5 seconds means 12 bongs in 10 | Bongs take longer than gaps, so 12 bongs must take 12 seconds
test: Zoe: "No cap, 2000 was the first year of the 21st century, everyone partied." What is right? | The partying is true, but the century began in 2001 | She's right, 2000 opened the century | The century began in 1999 | Centuries start with whatever year people pick
test: Rhys from Cardiff, tamping: "Clocks went forward and I lost my sleep! Bed at 10, alarm at 7, that's 9 hours, so why am I knackered?" What is the real answer? | He slept only 8 hours, because an hour was skipped | He slept 9 hours and is only pretending to be tired | He slept 10 hours, because the clocks went back that night | He slept 7 hours, because he lost two hours that night
test: Kaylee from Texas: "Y'all, I'm fixin' to call Grandma in Kyiv at 9 in the morning New York time, in January. Will she be up?" What is true? | Yes; it is 4 pm in Kyiv, the middle of the afternoon | No; it is 2 am in Kyiv and she is fast asleep | No; it is 9 pm the night before in Kyiv | Yes, it is 9 am for everyone everywhere at once
test: Sam, in 2026: "My cousin was born on 29 February 2016. She's only 2 lol, she's had two birthdays." What is wrong? | She is 10; she has had only 2 real 29 February birthdays | Nothing; leaplings get one year older every 4 years | She is 8, because 2016 had no 29 February | She is 2 at school and 10 only at home
test: In a cartoon an explorer digs up a coin stamped "25 BC" and shouts, "Priceless!" Why is the expert laughing? | Nobody in 25 BC knew to count years "BC", so it is a fake | Coins were never made before AD 1, as the Romans had none | 25 BC is after AD 1, so the date is impossible | Coins from that time are worth very little
test: Callum from Glasgow: "It's dreich and the clouds are thick, but the sundial in the park'll tell us the time, aye?" What is true? | No; with no sunshine there is no shadow to read | Yes; sundials work in any weather | Yes, but only if he shines a torch at the stone | No; sundials only work at night
test: Mia: "I'm 9 and my brother's 3, so I'm 3 times his age. In 3 years I'll still be 3 times as old, obvs." Is she right? | No; she'll be 12 and he'll be 6, only twice as old | Yes; 3 times as old stays the same for life | Yes; she'll be 18 and he'll be 6 | No; they will be the same age
exam: It is given that the present day is a Wednesday. Ascertain the day of the week which shall fall one hundred days hence. | Friday | Thursday | Saturday | Wednesday
exam: A clock occupies five seconds in striking six, the intervals between strokes being equal. Determine the time it occupies in striking twelve. | Eleven seconds | Ten seconds | Twelve seconds | Fourteen seconds
exam: A person born in the year 20 BC attains a birthday in AD 20. State the age then attained, there being no year zero. | Thirty-nine | Forty | Forty-one | Twenty
exam: Identify which of the following years shall contain no twenty-ninth day of February. | 2100 | 2028 | 2032 | 2400
